Let $G$ be a 3-connected cubic (3-regular) graph. A blue-red vertex coloring of $G$ is a partition $V(G) = V_B \cup V_R$ of its vertex set into two classes (blue and red); let $B$ and $R$ denote the subgraphs of $G$ induced by $V_B$ and $V_R$, respectively. Such a coloring is called crumby if it satisfies all three of the following conditions:
(i) $\Delta(B) \le 1$, i.e., the blue induced subgraph is a matching together with isolated vertices;
(ii) $\delta(R) \ge 1$, i.e., every red vertex has at least one red neighbor;
(iii) $R$ contains no path with 3 edges (i.e., no $P_4$ as a subgraph).

It is known that there exist 3-connected cubic graphs admitting no crumby coloring, so condition (iii) (combined with (i) and (ii)) cannot be guaranteed for every 3-connected cubic graph. 

Problem: Find a relaxation of the conditions imposed on the red induced subgraph $R$ — that is, a weaker property $\mathcal{P}(R)$ replacing the requirement "$\delta(R) \ge 1$ and $R$ contains no 3-edge path" — such that every 3-connected cubic graph $G$ admits a blue-red vertex coloring with $\Delta(B) \le 1$ and with $R$ satisfying $\mathcal{P}(R)$.

